      "markdown": "Our examples show that in matrix products the order of factors must always be observed very carefully. Otherwise matrix multiplication satisfies rules similar to those for numbers, namely.\n\n $$ (k\\mathbf{A})\\mathbf{B}=k(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{B})=\\mathbf{A}(k\\mathbf{B}) $$ \n\nwritten kAB or AkB\n\n $$ \\mathbf{A}(\\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{C})=(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{B})\\mathbf{C} $$ \n\nwritten ABC\n\n $$ (\\mathbf{A}+\\mathbf{B})\\mathbf{C}=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{C}+\\mathbf{B}\\mathbf{C} $$ \n\n $$ \\mathbf{C}(\\mathbf{A}+\\mathbf{B})=\\mathbf{C}\\mathbf{A}+\\mathbf{C}\\mathbf{B} $$ \n\nprovided A, B, and C are such that the expressions on the left are defined; here, k is any scalar. (2b) is called the associative law. (2c) and (2d) are called the distributive laws.",
